From bceaaba3e83d85438cdb9cb5d2713a6b73903436 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: rautshivamxyz-beep <rautshivamxyz@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 14 Nov 2026 03:59:46 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] v9.33.150 "A catch that can never run"

ChatStore.save stopped throwing at v9.33.90, and v9.33.148 gave it an answer
to return. Both were the right design, and both turned every

    try { ChatStore.save(act, currentChat) } catch (e: Exception) { NovaLog.quiet(...) }

in the chat engine into dead code. 32 of them were still there, across eight
files - every save the assistant makes on its own behalf: the clarification
turn, the entity reply, the refusal, the online answer, the exam and tutor
turns, the summary. The catch could never run, so the block only ever did one
thing: it swallowed CancellationException.

That is not cosmetic. A turn the user cancelled - or one cancelled because
they switched chats - carried on through the save and out the other side as
if it had finished, because a coroutine's cancellation was caught as an
Exception and answered with a log line. Coroutine cancellation is not an
error to be reported; it is the signal to stop.

All 32 wrappers are gone. The saves themselves stay exactly where they were,
in the same dispatchers, and they still record a failed write through
NcieFiles.writeOrNote - the tally the health screen reads. This is the same
class of dead code v9.33.147 removed from six files and v9.33.149 from the
chat screen; the engine was the rest of it.

Four verify checks hold the sweep: no `try { ChatStore.save` survives
anywhere, all 32 saves are still made, no file lost its save along with its
catch, and every touched file still balances.
